"Recent Tailscale vulnerability reports : r/Tailscale (https://www.reddit.com/r/Tailscale/comments/1xeoj1l/recent_tailscale_vulnerability_reports/)\nciteturn48view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.reddit.com/r/Tailscale/comments/1xeoj1l/recent_tailscale_vulnerability_reports/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 746\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: Open menu Open navigation Go to Reddit Home\nL3: \nL4: [Input: Search Reddit]\nL5: \nL6: 【1†Log In】 Log in to Reddit\nL7: \nL8: Expand user menu Open settings menu\nL9: \nL10: 【2†Go to Tailscale】\nL11: \nL12: 【2†r/Tailscale】\nL13: \nL14: • 1d ago\nL15: \nL16: 【3†u/shaver】\nL17: # Recent Tailscale vulnerability reports\nL18: \nL19: Hi folks, Mike Shaver here, VP of Engineering at Tailscale.\nL20: \nL21: We've seen the concerns about Tailscale SSH and wanted to share what we know so far.\nL22: \nL23: If you want to jump to the most important part, based on our current assessment, we're not recommending that everyone disable Tailscale SSH.\nL24: We've now received the researcher's report and are reviewing it. Our engineering and security teams had already been investigating independently, with help from external security specialists. That work has identified issues we're working to fix. We're still validating the full scope, including which configurations are affected.\nL25: \nL26: A bit of technical context:\nL27: Tailscale SSH uses your tailnet's access policies to control who can connect to which machines. Most of the issues we've identified so far concern what happens after someone has been authorized to connect, particularly how Unix privileges and SSH forwarding are handled. In some cases, a session may be able to do more than the configured local user should be allowed to do.\nL28: While it’s a serious concern, it isn't the same as an unauthenticated attacker being able to connect to arbitrary machines, but the potential impact depends on how SSH access is configured. We're still investigating, so we don't want to make blanket claims about what's affected or not.\nL29: \nL30: Again, based on our current assessment, we're not recommending that everyone disable Tailscale SSH.\nL31: \nL32: In the meantime, there are a few precautions you can take:\nL33:   * Review your SSH access policies. Make sure only the people and devices that need access have it.\nL34: \nL35:   * Disable SSH forwarding if you don't need it. You can set `TS_SSH_DISABLE_FORWARDING=1` in the `tailscaled` service environment and restart the service. This disables SSH forwarding (without disabling all of Tailscale SSH itself), which mitigates several of the more serious findings.\nL36:   * Consider enabling SSH check mode for an additional authentication step. This won't fix privilege escalation issues, but it does add another barrier to gaining access.\nL37: \nL38:   * Enable automatic updates where supported so you can pick up fixes when they're released.\nL39: \nL40: For particularly sensitive systems, consider temporarily tightening your Tailscale SSH access policies while we investigate, so that only the specific users and devices that need access can connect.\nL41: We're working on fixes and testing them carefully. We'll publish a security advisory explaining the confirmed findings, affected configurations, recommended actions and available patches. We'll also follow up with a more detailed technical write-up.\nL42: \nL43: If we find anything that changes our assessment or requires immediate customer action, we'll communicate that.\nL44: We know it's been a rough week for security teams, and we understand why people are concerned. We'd rather give you the technical context we have than speculate about things we haven't confirmed yet.\nL45: \nL46: We'll share more as we verify the details. If you have any questions about how this relates to your particular environment, please get in touch with our team 【4†https://tailscale.com/contact/support#support-form†tailscale.com】\nL47: \nL48: 【5†Share】\nL49: \nL50: Sort by:\nL51: \nL52: Best\nL53: \nL54: Open comment sort options\nL55: \nL56: * Best\nL57: \nL58: * Top\nL59: \nL60: * New\nL61: * Controversial\nL62: \nL63: * Old\nL64: \nL65: * Q&A\nL66: # Comments Section\nL67: \nL68: 【6†cloudsourced285】\nL69: \nL70: •\nL71: \nL72: 【7†1d ago】\nL73: \nL74: This is the sort or communication that helps grow confidence that security is taken seriously, especially as you point out, In times when tech teams are pretty exhausted. Good work on being upfront about what's going on, what you know and what you are doing.\nL75: \nL76: 【3†shaver】\nL77: \nL78: •\nL79: \nL80: 【8†1d ago】\nL81: \nL82: Thank you. We know that our users and customers place a tremendous amount of trust in us, and we take that responsibility very seriously indeed.\nL83: \nL84: 【9†mandreko】\nL85: \nL86: •\nL87: 【10†15h ago】\nL88: \nL89: Agree wholeheartedly. When I reported a security vulnerability, it was taken seriously, fixed within 24 hours, and communication was great. I’ve submitted CVEs for many companies and Tailscale was one of the top positive experiences.\nL90: \nL91: 【3†shaver】\nL92: \nL93: •\nL94: \nL95: 【11†9h ago】\nL96: \nL97: Just a comment to be transparent about my edit to the OP, changing the bullet about forwarding permissions to read\nL98: > Disable SSH forwarding if you don't need it. You can set `TS_SSH_DISABLE_FORWARDING=1` in the `tailscaled` service environment and restart the service. This disables SSH forwarding (without disabling all of Tailscale SSH itself), which mitigates several of the more serious findings.\nL99: \nL100: 【12†Denzh】\nL101: \nL102: •\nL103: \nL104: 【13†1d ago】\nL105: \nL106: For clarification, this is related to tailscale ssh, and not regular ssh with sshd - with clients made reachable by the tailscale wireguard tunnel, right?\nL107: \nL108: 【3†shaver】\nL109: \nL110: •\nL111: \nL112: 【14†17h ago】\nL113: that's correct\nL114: \nL115: 【15†linne000】\nL116: \nL117: •\nL118: \nL119: 【16†22h ago】\nL120: \nL121: I am only a Tailscale Insider, so not directly employed by Tailscale, fwiw.\nL122: \nL123: But yes, from what's been communicated it seems to be related to Tailscales own SSH feature, not regular SSH that happens to connect over a Tailscale tunnel.\nL124: \nL125: 【17†Seriel1】\nL126: \nL127: •\nL128: \nL129: 【18†20h ago】\nL130: \nL131: Correct\nL132: \nL133: 【19†cazwax】\nL134: \nL135: •\nL136: \nL137: 【20†1d ago】\nL138: \nL139: Is this -the- Mike Shaver of Moz of olde?\nL140: \nL141: 【3†shaver】\nL142: \nL143: •\nL144: \nL145: 【21†1d ago】\nL146: \nL147: sup, friend?\nL148: \nL149: 【19†cazwax】\nL150: \nL151: •\nL152: \nL153: 【22†1d ago】\nL154: \nL155: Nicely done old son\nL156: \nL157: 【23†Argon717】\nL158: \nL159: •\nL160: 【24†10h ago】\nL161: \nL162: I am just glad your okay based on my search autosuggest.\nL163: \nL164: 【25†retornam】\nL165: \nL166: •\nL167: \nL168: 【26†8m ago】\nL169: \nL170: 【3†u/shaver】 Mike \"Ten Fucking Days\" Shaver? I’ll never forget that. Hope you are doing well\nL171: \nL172: 【27†TheForce627】\nL173: \nL174: •\nL175: \nL176: 【28†1d ago】\nL177: \nL178: Customer service earns lifetime customers. This level of communication is very much appreciated.\nL179: \nL180: 【29†Sillygoat2】\nL181: \nL182: •\nL183: \nL184: 【30†1d ago】\nL185: \nL186: Thank you for treating your customers like actual adults who’s respect is earned. Why is this so difficult for (gestures broadly) everybody else?\nL187: 【31†esssssssss】\nL188: \nL189: •\nL190: \nL191: 【32†1d ago】\nL192: \nL193: Appreciate the open communication\nL194: \nL195: 【33†Turlte_Dicks_at_Work】\nL196: \nL197: •\nL198: \nL199: 【34†1d ago】\nL200: \nL201: I knew I liked you guys for a reason!\nL202: \nL203: 【35†anikansk】\nL204: \nL205: •\nL206: \nL207: 【36†1d ago】\nL208: \nL209: You guys are a breath of fresh air.\nL210: \nL211: 【37†biggobird】\nL212: \nL213: •\nL214: \nL215: 【38†1d ago】\nL216: \nL217: This kind of comms is why I recommended our family businesses onboard with Tailscale for secure remote access. In the process of doing so but this is a breath of fresh air\nL218: \nL219: 【39†VisualNinja1】\nL220: \nL221: •\nL222: \nL223: 【40†23h ago】\nL224: \nL225: 💯\nL226: \nL227: 【41†milyrouge】\nL228: \nL229: •\nL230: \nL231: 【42†23h ago】\nL232: Open, honest, actually helpful communication with users on a public forum? Wow! That’s rare in today’s world.\nL233: \nL234: Thanks for the update. Really appreciate it.\nL235: \nL236: 【43†VE3VVS】\nL237: \nL238: •\nL239: \nL240: 【44†1d ago】\nL241: \nL242: If more companies would be as open and transparent about potential risks or issues as you guys at Tailscale are, the industry would be a far safer and functional place. Thank you 🙏\nL243: \nL244: 【45†DitiPenguin】\nL245: \nL246: •\nL247: \nL248: 【46†16h ago】\nL249: \nL250: • Edited 11h ago\nL251: Don’t forget 【47†about telemetry†tailscale.com】! Tailscale are open and transparent after all, so you were definitely aware the telemetry is opt-out only, right? Right?\nL252: \nL253: (Their product is so amazing I’m not too mad about the telemetry, but it still is a huge dark pattern IMHO.)\nL254: \nL255: 【48†jcy】\nL256: \nL257: •\nL258: \nL259: 【49†14h ago】\nL260: \nL261: you meant to say opt-out here right\nL262: \nL263: 【50†thisChalkCrunchy】\nL264: \nL265: •\nL266: \nL267: 【51†11h ago】\nL268: \nL269: right? Right?\nL270: \nL271: 【52†more reply】\nL272: \nL273: 【45†DitiPenguin】\nL274: \nL275: •\nL276: \nL277: 【53†11h ago】\nL278: \nL279: Yes! Open-out is obviously a typo.\nL280: \nL281: 【54†d_t_s1997】\nL282: \nL283: •\nL284: 【55†1d ago】\nL285: \nL286: Always glad to read about this sort of things, i wish more companies and product owner would talk about their security issues like you guys doing.\nL287: \nL288: 【56†jeffrey_smith】\nL289: \nL290: •\nL291: \nL292: 【57†1d ago】\nL293: \nL294: Brilliant. Thanks for writing!\nL295: \nL296: 【58†lightspeedissueguy】\nL297: \nL298: •\nL299: \nL300: 【59†1d ago】\nL301: \nL302: Honest question since I don't use it, but why is TS ssh even a thing? Couldn't ACL allow select users to port 22, or does TS manage rbac? Or is it just a convenience thing?\nL303: \nL304: 【15†linne000】\nL305: \nL306: •\nL307: \nL308: 【60†22h ago】\nL309: It also replaces SSH keys needing to be managed. Basically your authenticated Tailscale identity is passed to the server and if you have access based on policy you are let in. This means that a change in the Tailscale ACL is all that's needed to control access without also needing to distribute keys or run a jump host etc.\nL310: \nL311: PS. For disclosure's sake, I am a Tailscale Insider\nL312: \nL313: 【61†Frosty_Scheme342】\nL314: \nL315: •\nL316: \nL317: 【62†19h ago】\nL318: There's a list of the benefits at 【63†https://tailscale.com/docs/features/tailscale-ssh#benefits†tailscale.com】\nL319: \nL320: 【64†as_i_wander】\nL321: \nL322: •\nL323: \nL324: 【65†1d ago】\nL325: \nL326: Appreciate the update\nL327: \nL328: 【66†xXbenihimeXx】\nL329: \nL330: •\nL331: \nL332: 【67†9h ago】\nL333: \nL334: très bon communiquer, j'attend que ca réagi sur ce problème, et vous êtes vraiment très réactif, rien que d'avoir des vrais informations cest un plus réel, très bon travail continuer comme sa👍♥️\nL335: \nL336: 【3†shaver】\nL337: \nL338: •\nL339: \nL340: 【68†9h ago】\nL341: \nL342: merci!\nL343: \nL344: 【69†JoshyWoshy7】\nL345: \nL346: •\nL347: \nL348: 【70†1d ago】\nL349: \nL350: Thanks for open comms and information!!\nL351: 【71†chaz6】\nL352: \nL353: •\nL354: \nL355: 【72†22h ago】\nL356: \nL357: Thanks for the update! Tailscale rocks.\nL358: \nL359: 【73†XCherryCokeO】\nL360: \nL361: •\nL362: \nL363: 【74†19h ago】\nL364: \nL365: YEAH!!!! GO TAILSCALE DUDE!!!\nL366: \nL367: 【75†Fesional】\nL368: \nL369: •\nL370: \nL371: 【76†1d ago】\nL372: \nL373: Is SSH off by default?\nL374: \nL375: 【77†kerbys_revenge】\nL376: \nL377: •\nL378: \nL379: 【78†1d ago】\nL380: \nL381: Yes you have to enable it\nL382: \nL383: 【79†Grimmoner】\nL384: \nL385: •\nL386: \nL387: 【80†1d ago】\nL388: 